    "case_id": "23",
    "case_summary": "A 53-year-old male from Mexico presented to the San Francisco General Hospital chest clinic with a history of chronic cough and pulmonary nodules. His respiratory symptoms began in 1985 with an intermittent cough that gradually became chronic and productive of yellow sputum, accompanied by mild shortness of breath. In 1997, he was treated with inhaled bronchodilators in Mexico with minimal relief. After immigrating to the United States in 2004, he was treated with a steroid inhaler by a primary care provider, also without relief. A chest radiograph was performed, leading to his referral to the chest clinic. During his visit, he reported no current fevers, chills, sweats, weight loss, eye pain, joint pain, rash, or sinusitis. He had no pets, mold exposure, recent travel, or environmental tobacco smoke exposure. He reported no other medical conditions and denied tobacco, alcohol, or illicit drug use. His occupational history included working at a charcoal production plant in Mexico from ages 18 to 26, where he spent 10-12 hours a day burning wood to make charcoal in a very smoky environment without using protective respiratory masks. He denied any mining, construction, or asbestos-related occupations. For the past year, he had been working as a dishwasher in a restaurant. On physical examination, he appeared healthy with normal vital signs and an oxygen saturation of 97%. The examination was normal except for bilateral forced expiratory wheezes. There were no signs of pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure, or clubbing. Routine laboratory tests, including a complete blood count and blood chemistry panel, were normal. Three sputum samples for mycobacteria showed no growth at 56 days. Serologies for fungal diseases were negative. Thoracic high-resolution computed axial tomography (HRCT) was performed using a 7 mm helical technique after intravenous contrast administration, followed by high-resolution imaging with 1 mm collimation every 1 cm from lung apex to base. Pulmonary function test results were: forced vital capacity (FVC) 3.46 L (91%), forced expiratory volume in 1 sec (FEV1) 2.31 L (74%), FEV1:FVC ratio 67, total lung capacity 5.27 L (95%), residual volume 1.57 L (91%), diffusion capacity for carbon monoxide 23.1 mL/mmHg/min (81%), and diffusion capacity for CO corrected for total lung capacity by single breath 4.61 mL/mmHg/L (87%). Arterial blood gases were normal.\n\nBronchoscopy demonstrated a large black and gray plaque in the left mainstem bronchus and multiple smaller gray and black deposits in the left upper lobe, left lower lobe, and right lower lobe. Transbronchial biopsies were obtained.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
